 heavy outside spending"], "rationale": "Raters overwhelmingly call this Likely D and markets price Vasquez near 90%, while his fundraising edge and incumbency support a clear but not safe advantage. The lone recent poll shows only a 3-point margin, so the expected margin stays modest rather than double-digit.", "watch": "Fresh polling or the flood of attack ads against Vasquez could tighten the race toward a genuine tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Vasquez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Gabe Vasquez defends a southern New Mexico swing seat that has flipped between parties in recent cycles. He holds a major financial edge, with $2.4 million on hand to Greg Cunningham's $800,000, and has drawn support from national figures while touting constituent services. Ratings from most analysts put the race in Likely D territory, though Cook and Sabato say Lean D and Inside Elections says Tilt D.\n\nAvailable polling is sparse: a September SurveyUSA survey showed Vasquez ahead by just three points, and a Republican-sponsored spring poll had the race even in a fully matched matchup. Prediction markets price Vasquez's chances between 89 and 95 percent. Attack ads have flooded the district, and national attention keeps this competitive, but the weight of evidence favors the incumbent by a moderate margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8892, "margin": 7.53}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8688, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8708, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.87, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8306, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8384, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8302, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8733, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8614, "margin": 7.0}]}